<BlogPost
  title="Gravity on Other Planets: A Comparative Guide"
  description="Explore how gravity differs across the solar system and what it would feel like to stand on other planets. From lightweight Mercury to crushing Jupiter."
  publishDate="2024-02-01"
  readTime="7 min read"
  keywords="planetary gravity, gravity comparison, Mars gravity, Jupiter gravity, solar system"
>
  <p>
    Earth's gravity feels normal to us because we evolved here, but it's just one data point in a diverse solar
    system. Each planet has its own gravitational pull determined by its mass and radius, creating vastly different
    experiences for hypothetical visitors.
  </p>

  <h2>How Planetary Gravity Works</h2>
  <p>
    Surface gravity depends on two factors: a planet's mass (more mass means stronger gravity) and its radius
    (larger radius means you're farther from the center, weakening gravity at the surface). The interplay of
    these factors creates surprising variations.
  </p>

  <h3>The Basic Formula</h3>
  <p>
    Surface gravity is proportional to mass divided by radius squared. This means a planet can be massive but
    still have weak surface gravity if it's also very large, or small but have strong gravity if it's extremely
    dense.
  </p>

  <h2>Gravity Tour of the Solar System</h2>

  <h3>Mercury: The Lightweight</h3>
  <p>
    Surface gravity: 38% of Earth's. On Mercury, you could jump nearly three times higher than on Earth, and
    everything would feel much lighter. A 100-pound person would weigh just 38 pounds—though the 430°C daytime
    temperatures might be more immediately concerning than the light gravity.
  </p>

  <h3>Venus: Nearly Identical</h3>
  <p>
    Surface gravity: 91% of Earth's. Despite Venus being Earth's "evil twin" with crushing atmospheric pressure
    and sulfuric acid clouds, its gravity is nearly identical to ours. You'd barely notice the difference in
    weight.
  </p>

  <h3>Mars: The Settlement Target</h3>
  <p>
    Surface gravity: 38% of Earth's. Mars has the same surface gravity as Mercury, despite being larger—Mercury
    is much denser. This presents challenges for future Mars colonists, who would need to deal with long-term
    health effects of reduced gravity.
  </p>

  <h3>Jupiter: The Crusher</h3>
  <p>
    Surface gravity: 254% of Earth's. Jupiter is so massive that despite being a gas giant with no solid surface,
    its "surface" gravity (at the top of its atmosphere) would make you weigh two and a half times more. Standing
    would be exhausting, and jumping would be nearly impossible.
  </p>

  <h3>Saturn: Surprisingly Weak</h3>
  <p>
    Surface gravity: 107% of Earth's. Saturn is the solar system's second-largest planet but has very weak gravity
    for its size because it's the least dense planet. It's so light it would float in water (if you could find
    an ocean big enough).
  </p>

  <h3>Uranus and Neptune</h3>
  <p>
    Surface gravity: About 89% and 114% of Earth's respectively. These ice giants have surprisingly moderate
    gravity—Neptune feels slightly heavier than Earth, while Uranus feels slightly lighter.
  </p>

  <h2>The Moon: Earth's Lighter Companion</h2>
  <p>
    Surface gravity: 17% of Earth's. The Moon's weak gravity led to the famous bouncing astronaut walks during
    Apollo missions. Everything weighs only one-sixth of its Earth weight, making movement require completely
    different techniques.
  </p>

  <h2>Health Effects of Different Gravity</h2>
  <p>
    Long-term exposure to different gravity levels affects human health:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Reduced gravity causes bone density loss and muscle atrophy</li>
    <li>High gravity would strain the cardiovascular system</li>
    <li>Balance and coordination systems would need to recalibrate</li>
    <li>Pregnancy and child development in altered gravity is unexplored</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Implications for Space Exploration</h2>
  <p>
    Understanding planetary gravity is crucial for:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Designing spacecraft landing and takeoff systems</li>
    <li>Planning crew health and exercise regimens</li>
    <li>Engineering habitats and equipment for different worlds</li>
    <li>Choosing settlement locations (Mars vs. Moon vs. asteroids)</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <strong>Key Takeaway:</strong> Gravity varies dramatically across the solar system, from barely-there Mercury
    and the Moon to crushing Jupiter. These differences aren't just curiosities—they fundamentally affect how we
    could explore and potentially settle other worlds, with implications for engineering, biology, and human health.
  </p>
